      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND AND OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "Epidural morphine with fentanyl has been used with some success to treat the pain associated with Guillain-Barré syndrome. This report describes a female with Guillain-Barré syndrome in which lower extremity and generalized positional pain failed to respond to conventional pain therapy. An epidural infusion of bupivacaine and fentanyl was used to treat her pain."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "A case report."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "An epidural infusion of 15 mg/hour bupivacaine and 75 micrograms/hour fentanyl produced good pain relief (visual analog scale decreased from 9 to 2) and allowed increased mobility. Over 24 days of treatment, the infusion requirements gradually increased, suggestive of tachyphylaxis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "These patients sometimes fail to respond to conventional pain therapy and may require the expertise of an anesthesiologist to deliver epidural analgesics."
        }
      ]},
